+<p>
+  When a server allows a search parameter to be supplied in the URL of a POST search, there is no semantic
+  difference between supplying that parameter in the URL and supplying it in the body. Parameters accepted from
+  both locations SHALL be combined into a single set of search parameters; for repeating parameters, this is the
+  same as repeating the parameter within one location.
+</p>
 <p>
   Note that servers MAY impose restrictions on what parameters are allowed to be passed as query parameters on POST
   requests <span class="stu">TU</span>.  For example, a server could allow the <code>_format</code> query parameter
-  but reject searches with a <code>Patient.name</code> query parameter.
+  but reject searches with a <code>Patient.name</code> query parameter. Servers MAY also ignore query parameters on
+  POST requests that they do not support, in which case the <a href="search.html#selflink">self link</a> SHALL
+  reflect the parameters that were actually used to process the search.
 </p>
 
